{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And behold, three men.</b> One to bring the tidings to Sarah, and one to overturn Sodom, and one to heal Abraham — for a single angel does not perform two missions (Bereshit Rabbah). Know this for yourself: the entire passage refers to them in plural language — וַיֹּאכֵלוּ, וַיֹּאמְרוּ אֵלָיו — and in the tidings it says ״וַיֹּאמֶר שׁוֹב אָשׁוּב אֵלֶיךָ״, and in the overturning of Sodom it says ״כִּי לֹא אוּכַל לַעֲשׂוֹת דָּבָר לְבִלְתִּי הַפְכִּי״, and Raphael, who healed Abraham, went from there to save Lot; as it says ״וַיְהִי כְהוֹצִיאָם אֹתָם הַחוּצָה וַיֹּאמֶר הִמָּלֵט עַל נַפְשֶׁךָ״ — you learn from this that one of them was the savior (Bereshit Rabbah)." "<b>Standing over him.</b> Before him — but it is refined language out of respect for the angels." "<b>And he saw.</b> What is וַיַּרְא — וַיַּרְא twice? The first is as it sounds, and the second is a term of understanding: he perceived that they were standing in one place and understood that they did not wish to trouble him. And even though they knew he would come out to meet them, they stood in their place for his honor, to show him that they did not wish to trouble him — and he anticipated and ran to meet them. In Bava Metzi'a it is written ״נִצָּבִים עָלָיו״ and it is written ״וַיָּרָץ לִקְרָאתָם״? When they saw that he was beginning to untie [his bandage], they stepped back from him; immediately — ״וַיָּרָץ לִקְרָאתָם״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {2 "The Aramaic clause כַּד חַזְיוּהוּ... is from Bava Metzi'a 86b, explaining that the angels stepped away when they saw Abraham in pain untying his circumcision bandage."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:59:12.825037835Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}